WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.660
Bacteria get around. They move in
sometimes mysterious ways.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.559
One of the most mysterious is via the pilus. To get
ahead using pili, a pilus is thrown

00:00:12.559 --> 00:00:17.680
forward, like a grappling hook. After
touchdown, the pilus is retracted back and

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.740
the cel is dragged along.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.580
But how does
the cell make a grappling hook and pull it back in?

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.930
Researchers used electron cryotomography to take many many pictures of

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:33.030
the pilus machinery. Then using genetic
techniques they took away some

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:38.250
components and they labeled others, mapping the location of each protein in the structure.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.260
With this new understanding of
the structure, they proposed a mechanism

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.860
for how the pilus gets sent out and
brought back.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:51.880
When activated, a motor-like protein adds  units together into a chain,

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:54.560
which pushes out through the membrane.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.080
When the extending chain touches the
surface, the proteins change their shape--

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:04.440
sending a message back to the machinery. 
In response to this message,

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.400
a different motor reverses the process--
retracting the pilus and pulling the cell

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:10.470
along.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.920
Taking into account the size and forces at
work here,

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.960
the pilus motor is probably one of the
strongest molecular motors around.

